    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d673">

                <pb facs="0652" n="642" sameAs="frus:frus1941v05/pg642"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.24/1122</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Lauchlin Currie,
                        Administrative Assistant to President Roosevelt, to the Secretary of State</hi><anchor n="62"
                        xml:id="d673-f1"/>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1941-05-03">May 3, 1941</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p rendition="#center">
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Memorandum for the Secretary of State</hi>:</p>
                <p rendition="#flush">Re: <hi rendition="#italic">Communications with
                        Chungking.</hi>
                </p>
                <p>In accordance with your suggestion, I discussed this matter with Dr. Hornbeck.
                    With reference to communications by private code between the President and
                    Chiang Kai-shek, we decided that although it would be preferable to have all
                    messages go through regular diplomatic channels, still, in this instance, it
                    seemed advisable to reply to Chiang Kai-shek’s message in his code through
                    commercial channels. Otherwise, he might feel rebuffed and embarrassed. Dr.
                    Hornbeck <pb facs="0653" n="643" xml:id="pg643"/> assisted in the drafting of
                    the attached message<anchor n="63" xml:id="d673-f2"/> which was coded in the
                    State Department’s code room and approved by the President. If there should be
                    any further messages, I shall show them to you immediately and consult you on
                    any replies.</p>
                <p>With reference to my own communications with Major McHugh, Naval Attaché at
                    Chungking, on matters pertaining to lease-lend aid for China, we decided that
                    for the time being I might continue to use naval radio, but that messages to and
                    from should be immediately communicated to you at this end, and to Ambassador
                        Gauss<anchor n="64" xml:id="d673-f3"/> at the other. I think it would be
                    desirable for you to inform the Ambassador of this arrangement if you
                    approve.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Lauchlin Currie</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d673-f1" n="62" type="source" xml:id="d673-f1-content">
                <p>Forwarded on May 5 to the Adviser on Political Relations (Hornbeck) with a
                    request for transmission to the Secretary of State.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d673-f2" n="63" xml:id="d673-f2-content">
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Supra.</hi>
                </p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d673-f3" n="64" xml:id="d673-f3-content">
                <p>Clarence E. Gauss, appointed Ambassador to China to succeed Nelson Trusler
                    Johnson.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
